---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeTotp from '@/components/tools/security/DevEncodeTotp.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="totp-generator">
  <DevEncodeTotp client:load mode="totp" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How a six-digit TOTP code is made</h2>
    <p>
      TOTP is RFC 6238, a thin layer over HOTP from RFC 4226. The algorithm has four steps:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Take the current Unix time in seconds and divide it by the step, normally 30. That integer is the counter.</li>
      <li>Write the counter as an 8-byte big-endian value and compute its HMAC-SHA1 with the shared secret.</li>
      <li>
        Take the low four bits of the last byte as an offset, read four bytes from that offset, and clear the
        top bit. This &ldquo;dynamic truncation&rdquo; turns 160 bits into 31.
      </li>
      <li>Take that number modulo 10⁶ and pad it to six digits.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      All of these steps run in this tab, with the browser's native WebCrypto for the HMAC. When the countdown
      ends, the code changes, and no request is made.
    </p>

    <h2>Why local execution matters for 2FA secrets</h2>
    <p>
      A 2FA secret is the whole second factor. If a website sends it anywhere, the second factor is
      compromised, and a visitor cannot tell from the interface what a site does with it.
    </p>
    <p>
      So test this page. Open DevTools, clear the Network panel and let the code change: no request appears.
      Turn off Wi-Fi and reload: the page still works. The response headers have a Content-Security-Policy with
      no third-party origin in <code>connect-src</code>, so the browser would block an upload even if the code
      tried one.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading the otpauth URI</h2>
    <pre><code>otpauth://totp/Example:alice@example.com
  ?secret=JBSWY3DPEHPK3PXP
  &amp;issuer=Example
  &amp;algorithm=SHA1
  &amp;digits=6
  &amp;period=30</code></pre>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Practical note</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>secret</code></td><td>The shared key, Base32</td><td>The only secret part</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>issuer</code></td><td>Service name</td><td>Shown as the heading in the app; also repeated in the label</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>algorithm</code></td><td>SHA1, SHA256 or SHA512</td><td>Some authenticator apps ignore anything but SHA1</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>digits</code></td><td>6, 7 or 8</td><td>Most apps ignore 7 and 8</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>period</code></td><td>Seconds per step</td><td>30 almost everywhere; some banks use 60</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      A QR code is that string drawn as a bitmap. Scanning it transfers the URI and nothing else.
    </p>

    <h2>When codes are rejected</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Clock skew: TOTP depends on the time. A device more than about 30 seconds off fails. Servers usually
        accept ±1 step, and this page verifies with the same tolerance.
      </li>
      <li>
        Wrong algorithm: if a service issued a SHA-256 secret and your app assumes SHA-1, every code is wrong
        and nothing tells you why.
      </li>
      <li>
        Secret typed by hand: Base32 excludes 0, 1 and 8 because people mistype them. If your secret contains
        one of those digits, it is not valid Base32.
      </li>
      <li>
        Code already used: well-built servers reject a code used a second time, even inside its window.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What the TOTP generator is for</h2>
    <p>
      Use it to test a 2FA integration, check whether a secret is valid, generate a key for a service you
      build, or read a code from a secret you already have. It is not an authenticator app: it stores nothing,
      so a refresh clears everything. Keep your real secrets in a dedicated app or hardware key, and keep the
      recovery codes in a different place.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
